From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lena <lena@x>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 01:10:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] lena-0156: show where the spawn stalls, name the lock holder

The 0155 boot stops printing right after

    exec launchd_cache_loader

and then sits idle for the whole 600 s of heartbeats: no UFS command, no
process dump, no panic. That line is printed by process_signature(), which
runs *after* the image is loaded, at the end of posix_spawn() - so the hang
is in the signature/spawn tail, not in the exec itself.

Three things now make the next boot answer it:

1. Spawn/exec markers: SWEET SPAWN/EXEC/SIG lines around exec_activate_image,
   exec_mach_imgact (entry, load_machfile, vm_map_exec, done),
   process_signature (CS_SIGNED decision, task access port, the taskgated
   upcall) and the task_terminate_internal() of the old task. The last marker
   of the next log names the call that never returns.

2. The process dump no longer blocks. 0155 produced not one SWEET PS line in
   600 s while the heartbeat kept printing, which means the dump thread was
   parked - most likely in proc_list_lock() or in a process's p_mlock. It now
   uses lck_mtx_try_lock(): on failure it prints who holds the lock and skips
   the round, and prints "dump N start" before every walk so a walk that
   begins and never ends is visible.

3. proc_list_lock() records its holder (thread, pid, caller return address,
   acquisition time) and complains once a second when a waiter has to block
   that long. The holder's site address can be symbolized offline. msleep()
   over the same mutex bypasses the bookkeeping, so the recorded holder is
   advisory, not gospel.

Also fixes the ring flush position: it is advanced to a block boundary, so
after a flush it can be up to one block past `total`; the next flush computed
total - flushed, wrapped to ~2^64, and set pos = total - 4 MiB - an impossible
position that skipped the data copy and stored that value as the advertised
total (the 0155 pull read total = 18446744073705393439 = 36127 - 4 MiB). Both
positions are now clamped to `total`, which also removes the ~1.7 KiB hole
where a partially filled block was persisted once and never rewritten (33
heartbeats of the 0155 boot were lost that way).
